      "problem": "Solve $x^3+x^2-9x-9=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-9x-9 & = \\\\ ({x}^{3}+{x}^{2})-(9x+9) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+1)-9(x+1) & = \\\\ (x+1)\\cdot {x}^{2}-(x+1)\\cdot 9 & = \\\\ (x+1)({x}^{2}-9) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(x+1)(x^2-9) = 0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(x+1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(x^2-9)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-1, \\, \\pm  3.$",
